= Solution
{parent=a}

By the <spectral radius formula>, $r(T)<1$. Choose
$$
r(T)<r<s<1.
$$
The formula gives a constant $C$ such that $\lVert T^n\rVert\leq Cr^n$ for every $n$. Since $f$ is holomorphic on the unit disc, the <Cauchy estimate> on the circle of radius $s$ gives $|a_n|\leq M_s s^{-n}$. Therefore
$$
\sum_{n\geq0}|a_n|\lVert T^n\rVert
\leq CM_s\sum_{n\geq0}(r/s)^n<\infty.
$$
**Thus $\sum_{n\geq0}a_nT^n$ converges absolutely in operator norm.**

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If $T$ is normal, then $S=T-I$ is normal and $\sigma(S)=\{0\}$. The <C-star identity> and normality imply $\lVert S^n\rVert=\lVert S\rVert^n$. The <spectral radius formula> consequently gives
$$
\lVert S\rVert=r(S)=0,
$$
so $T=I$.

Normality is essential. On $\ell^2$, let $N$ send $e_2$ to $e_1$ and every other basis vector to zero. Then $N\ne0$ but $N^2=0$, so
$$
\sigma(I+N)=1+\sigma(N)=\{1\},
$$
while $I+N\ne I$.

